ions and see if we can make  a little bit of sense out of that  yeah that's a great question so if you  look at our chimpanzee cousins they're  about 500 times as likely to come into  physical conflict with other members of  their group than humans are and so when  we look at human hunter-gatherers so  there's no police to stop you no  government we see a ratio of 500 to one  for every one conflict we have where we  fight with each other over something  they have 500 but if you look at our  chimpanzee cousins when they interact  with members of other groups now you see  a one-to-one ratio they're exactly as  conflicted with members of other groups  as we are or we are exactly as much as  they  and so basically we've we've evolved to  be hyper cooperative we work together  incredibly well but that's primarily  within our groups  when we're looking at between group  relationships they look a lot like  chimpanzees did and so human males in  particular are the ones who when the  conflict comes they tend to get into it  but if you look at us within our groups  although we form shallower relationships  than human females do on average those  shallow relationships allow us to kind  of form coalitions that dissipate and  then that reform later without any hard  feelings but those coalitions almost  never get foreign when we cross group  boundaries and that's where we see the  real violence that's part of our nature  is the explanation for this simply that  if you work together rather than fight  with each other everybody's more likely  to survive both as a species and as a  community and as an individual  well yes and and the reason that the  chimpanzees never crossed that  particular rubicon is they never got to  the point that we got to on the savannah  where there was enormous benefit to be  gained if everybody would cooperate and  so when you look at chimpanzee  psychology they're very clever they do  lots of things very well but they're  fundamentally competitive with 