kes leadership effective in  hunter-gatherer societies is that it's  very distributed and it's very  egalitarian  we shifted away from that when we moved  to um when we became agriculturalists  because now we can store food and create  stable coalitions etc but interestingly  the modern world is shifting back to  that and one way to look at this  disaster in ukraine is that you've got a  paramount leader who nobody's told no to  for a very long time and so it's harder  for him to create and assess whether an  idea is good than it would be for  somebody who's democratically elected  who who knows that they're at the will  the people they could come and go and  their ideas are more important and they  have to be more persuasive  so  does this uh i mean jared diamond has  written a little bit about you know when  the when the agricultural revolution  happened  you if you want to think about it very  mathematically all of a sudden there  were p people who started to be  responsible for producing way more  calories than they needed  and that caloric surplus sort of opened  the door to specialization which  includes  people who  raise kids and also people who think and  people who do politics and think about  law and that type of thing is that  caloric surplus the the beginning of  sort of the modern idea of leadership  and politicians  very much so and so once we became  sedentary and we could create a caloric  surplus that we could store you have to  be like i could create a caloric surplus  on the equator by killing a giraffe  right but it's going to be gone that's a  key point yeah right so being able to  store it is a major thing yeah exactly  so once we get to that point where we  can store it we see society shifts  dramatically now agriculture enabled  that for most humans but it wasn't  always agriculture there for example  hunter-gatherers in the pacific  northwest knew where these big salmon  runs were going to come through and so  they could create coalitions t